===Results by oblast===
Poroshenko finished first in every region in which voting took place<ref>{{Cite web | title=Öåíòðàëüíà âèáîð÷à êîì³ñ³ÿ - ²ÀÑ "Âèáîðè Ïðåçèäåíòà Óêðà¿íè" | url=https://www.cvk.gov.ua/pls/vp2014/wp001.html | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.cvk.gov.ua}}</ref>, becoming the first [[Ukrainian presidential elections|Ukrainian presidential candidate]] to do so. His support was strongest in [[Western Ukraine|western]] and [[Central Ukraine|central ]][[Ukraine]]: he received more than 60% of the vote in [[Lviv Oblast|Lviv]], [[Vinnytsia Oblast|Vinnytsia]], [[Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast|Ivano-Frankivsk]], [[Ternopil Oblast|Ternopil]], [[Zakarpattia Oblast|Zakarpattia]] and [[Kyiv Oblast|Kyiv]] [[Oblasts of Ukraine|oblasts]], as well as in the [[Kyiv|city of Kyiv]] and among the [[Foreign electoral district of Ukraine|overseas voters]]. In the remaining western oblasts, and across most of northern and central Ukraine, his support generally ranged from the low to high 50s. His weakest results were recorded in the [[Eastern Ukraine|east]], including Luhansk, Kharkiv, Donetsk and Zaporizhzhia, where he received under 40% of the vote. Elsewhere in [[Southern Ukraine|southern]] and eastern Ukraine, his support generally ranged from the low to high 40s. The result therefore represented a substantial weakening of the east–west electoral divide seen in previous presidential elections, although significant regional differences in support remained<ref>{{Cite web | title=Ukraine's 2014 Presidential Election Result is Unlikely to be Repeated - PONARS Eurasia | url=https://www.ponarseurasia.org/ukraine-s-2014-presidential-election-result-is-unlikely-to-be-repeated/ | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210420201835/https://www.ponarseurasia.org/ukraine-s-2014-presidential-election-result-is-unlikely-to-be-repeated/ | access-date=2026-09-03 | archive-date=2021-04-20}}</ref>.

[[Yulia Tymoshenko|Tymoshenko]] and [[Oleh Liashko|Liashko]] both performed strongest in [[Western Ukraine|western]], northern and central Ukraine, particularly in more rural areas<ref>{{Cite web | title=Öåíòðàëüíà âèáîð÷à êîì³ñ³ÿ - ²ÀÑ "Âèáîðè Ïðåçèäåíòà Óêðà¿íè" | url=https://www.cvk.gov.ua/pls/vp2014/wp001.html | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.cvk.gov.ua}}</ref>. Tymoshenko recorded her best results in [[Chernihiv Oblast|Chernihiv]], [[Chernivtsi Oblast|Chernivtsi]], [[Volyn Oblast|Volyn]] and [[Khmelnytskyi Oblast|Khmelnytskyi]] [[Oblasts of Ukraine|oblasts]], while Liashko was strongest in his home region of Chernihiv, as well as Volyn and [[Rivne Oblast|Rivne]].

By contrast, Tihipko's support was concentrated in [[Southern Ukraine|southern]] and [[Eastern Ukraine|eastern]] [[Ukraine]], particularly in [[Donetsk Oblast|Donetsk]], [[Odesa Oblast|Odesa]], [[Luhansk Oblast|Luhansk]], [[Zaporizhzhia Oblast|Zaporizhzhia]] and [[Mykolaiv Oblast|Mykolaiv]] [[Oblasts of Ukraine|oblasts]]<ref>{{Cite web | title=Öåíòðàëüíà âèáîð÷à êîì³ñ³ÿ - ²ÀÑ "Âèáîðè Ïðåçèäåíòà Óêðà¿íè" | url=https://www.cvk.gov.ua/pls/vp2014/wp001.html | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.cvk.gov.ua}}</ref>. Dobkin's support was even more geographically concentrated, reaching over 26% in his home region of [[Kharkiv Oblast|Kharkiv]], where he finished second<ref>{{Cite web | title=Öåíòðàëüíà âèáîð÷à êîì³ñ³ÿ - ²ÀÑ "Âèáîðè Ïðåçèäåíòà Óêðà¿íè" | url=https://www.cvk.gov.ua/pls/vp2014/wp001.html | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.cvk.gov.ua}}</ref>.

[[Voter turnout]] also varied sharply by region<ref>{{Cite web | title=Öåíòðàëüíà âèáîð÷à êîì³ñ³ÿ - ²ÀÑ "Âèáîðè Ïðåçèäåíòà Óêðà¿íè" | url=https://www.cvk.gov.ua/pls/vp2014/wp001.html | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.cvk.gov.ua}}</ref>. Across much of western Ukraine it ranged from the low to high 70s, while most northern and central oblasts recorded turnout in the low to high 60s. Participation was considerably lower in the south and east: it fell below 50% in [[Odesa Oblast|Odesa]] and [[Kharkiv Oblast|Kharkiv]], while most other oblasts in these regions recorded turnout in the low to mid-50s. Turnout was exceptionally low in [[Donetsk Oblast|Donetsk]] (15.10%) and [[Luhansk Oblast|Luhansk]] (38.94%), where voting was severely disrupted by the ongoing armed conflict<ref>{{Cite web | title=Monitoring Ukraine's presidential election {{!}} OSCE Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights | url=https://odihr.osce.org/odihr/elections/ukraine/121163 | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=odihr.osce.org | date=11 July 2014 }}</ref>.

Lower participation in the [[Eastern Ukraine|east]] and [[Southern Ukraine|south]] also occurred against a background of considerably greater distrust of the post-[[Revolution of Dignity|Maidan]] authorities. An April 2014 Pew Research Center survey found that only 24% of respondents in eastern Ukraine viewed the influence of the Kyiv government positively, compared with 60% in the west, while only 27% of eastern respondents expected the presidential election to be conducted fairly, compared with 59% in the west<ref>{{Cite web | title=Presidential vote may not bridge regional divide in Ukraine {{!}} Pew Research Center | url=https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2014/05/22/presidential-vote-may-not-bridge-regional-divide-in-ukraine/ | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.pewresearch.org | date=22 May 2014 }}</ref>. These figures indicated substantial political alienation in parts of the east and south, although the particularly low turnout in Donetsk and Luhansk was also directly affected by violence and the inability to conduct voting across much of the two regions<ref>{{Cite web | title=Despite Concerns about Governance, Ukrainians Want to Remain One Country {{!}} Pew Research Center | url=https://www.pewresearch.org/global/2014/05/08/despite-concerns-about-governance-ukrainians-want-to-remain-one-country/ | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.pewresearch.org | date=8 May 2014 }}</ref>.

